**Read:** [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP), particle counters and data acquisition; [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), section 4.5.6.

A controller acts on measurements with location, response, sampling and calibration limits. Room pressure does not uniquely determine filter integrity; particle count does not uniquely determine molecular contamination; a remote temperature does not necessarily track the product.

The Berkeley experiment used different counter flows and reporting intervals. Its stored one-minute averages did not retain instantaneous maxima. A brief event can therefore be physically important while contributing modestly to an average.

**Work:** A hypothetical signal is 10 particles/m³ for 59 seconds and 1000 particles/m³ for one second. Compute its one-minute average and compare it with its peak and integrated exposure. Design a logging and sensor-placement account that can test the event relevant to the process.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
